  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z in [−2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so overshooting starts there. Steps along x grow without bound once the learning rate is above 0.5.",
    "Each step is −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean 0 and standard deviation 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, so it is exactly 0 at B = 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 2× the step length, and the readout gives the true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss goes above 8 (diverged). The trail holds the start plus at most 40 landing points, and so does the ghost.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence from the start. 'New run, keep last path' moves on to new noise values.",
    "On screen, positions are capped at the edge of the bowl. The Loss readout always shows the true value."
  ]
}